;; /*
;; Package ppc64 implements a PPC64 assembler that assembles Go asm into
;; the corresponding PPC64 instructions as defined by the Power ISA 3.0B.
;; 
;; This document provides information on how to write code in Go assembler
;; for PPC64, focusing on the differences between Go and PPC64 assembly language.
;; It assumes some knowledge of PPC64 assembler. The original implementation of
;; PPC64 in Go defined many opcodes that are different from PPC64 opcodes, but
;; updates to the Go assembly language used mnemonics that are mostly similar if not
;; identical to the PPC64 mneumonics, such as VMX and VSX instructions. Not all detail
;; is included here; refer to the Power ISA document if interested in more detail.
;; 
;; Starting with Go 1.15 the Go objdump supports the -gnu option, which provides a
;; side by side view of the Go assembler and the PPC64 assembler output. This is
;; extremely helpful in determining what final PPC64 assembly is generated from the
;; corresponding Go assembly.
;; 
;; In the examples below, the Go assembly is on the left, PPC64 assembly on the right.
;; 
;; 1. Operand ordering
;; 
;; In Go asm, the last operand (right) is the target operand, but with PPC64 asm,
;; the first operand (left) is the target. The order of the remaining operands is
;; not consistent: in general opcodes with 3 operands that perform math or logical
;; operations have their operands in reverse order. Opcodes for vector instructions
;; and those with more than 3 operands usually have operands in the same order except
;; for the target operand, which is first in PPC64 asm and last in Go asm.
;; 
;; Example:
;; 
;; ADD R3, R4, R5		<=>	add r5, r4, r3
;; 
;; 2. Constant operands
;; 
;; In Go asm, an operand that starts with '$' indicates a constant value. If the
;; instruction using the constant has an immediate version of the opcode, then an
;; immediate value is used with the opcode if possible.
;; 
;; Example:
;; 
;; ADD $1, R3, R4		<=> 	addi r4, r3, 1
;; 
;; 3. Opcodes setting condition codes
;; 
;; In PPC64 asm, some instructions other than compares have variations that can set
;; the condition code where meaningful. This is indicated by adding '.' to the end
;; of the PPC64 instruction. In Go asm, these instructions have 'CC' at the end of
;; the opcode. The possible settings of the condition code depend on the instruction.
;; CR0 is the default for fixed-point instructions; CR1 for floating point; CR6 for
;; vector instructions.
;; 
;; Example:
;; 
;; ANDCC R3, R4, R5		<=>	and. r5, r3, r4 (set CR0)
;; 
;; 4. Loads and stores from memory
;; 
;; In Go asm, opcodes starting with 'MOV' indicate a load or store. When the target
;; is a memory reference, then it is a store; when the target is a register and the
;; source is a memory reference, then it is a load.
;; 
;; MOV{B,H,W,D} variations identify the size as byte, halfword, word, doubleword.
;; 
;; Adding 'Z' to the opcode for a load indicates zero extend; if omitted it is sign extend.
;; Adding 'U' to a load or store indicates an update of the base register with the offset.
;; Adding 'BR' to an opcode indicates byte-reversed load or store, or the order opposite
;; of the expected endian order. If 'BR' is used then zero extend is assumed.
;; 
;; Memory references n(Ra) indicate the address in Ra + n. When used with an update form
;; of an opcode, the value in Ra is incremented by n.
;; 
;; Memory references (Ra+Rb) or (Ra)(Rb) indicate the address Ra + Rb, used by indexed
;; loads or stores. Both forms are accepted. When used with an update then the base register
;; is updated by the value in the index register.
;; 
;; Examples:
;; 
;; MOVD (R3), R4		<=>	ld r4,0(r3)
;; MOVW (R3), R4		<=>	lwa r4,0(r3)
;; MOVWZU 4(R3), R4		<=>	lwzu r4,4(r3)
;; MOVWZ (R3+R5), R4		<=>	lwzx r4,r3,r5
;; MOVHZ  (R3), R4		<=>	lhz r4,0(r3)
;; MOVHU 2(R3), R4		<=>	lhau r4,2(r3)
;; MOVBZ (R3), R4		<=>	lbz r4,0(r3)
;; 
;; MOVD R4,(R3)		<=>	std r4,0(r3)
;; MOVW R4,(R3)		<=>	stw r4,0(r3)
;; MOVW R4,(R3+R5)		<=>	stwx r4,r3,r5
;; MOVWU R4,4(R3)		<=>	stwu r4,4(r3)
;; MOVH R4,2(R3)		<=>	sth r4,2(r3)
;; MOVBU R4,(R3)(R5)		<=>	stbux r4,r3,r5
;; 
;; 4. Compares
;; 
;; When an instruction does a compare or other operation that might
;; result in a condition code, then the resulting condition is set
;; in a field of the condition register. The condition register consists
;; of 8 4-bit fields named CR0 - CR7. When a compare instruction
;; identifies a CR then the resulting condition is set in that field
;; to be read by a later branch or isel instruction. Within these fields,
;; bits are set to indicate less than, greater than, or equal conditions.
;; 
;; Once an instruction sets a condition, then a subsequent branch, isel or
;; other instruction can read the condition field and operate based on the
;; bit settings.
;; 
;; Examples:
;; 
;; CMP R3, R4			<=>	cmp r3, r4	(CR0 assumed)
;; CMP R3, R4, CR1		<=>	cmp cr1, r3, r4
;; 
;; Note that the condition register is the target operand of compare opcodes, so
;; the remaining operands are in the same order for Go asm and PPC64 asm.
;; When CR0 is used then it is implicit and does not need to be specified.
;; 
;; 5. Branches
;; 
;; Many branches are represented as a form of the BC instruction. There are
;; other extended opcodes to make it easier to see what type of branch is being
;; used.
;; 
;; The following is a brief description of the BC instruction and its commonly
;; used operands.
;; 
;; BC op1, op2, op3
;; 
;; op1: type of branch
;; 16 -> bctr (branch on ctr)
;; 12 -> bcr  (branch if cr bit is set)
;; 8  -> bcr+bctr (branch on ctr and cr values)
;; 4  -> bcr != 0 (branch if specified cr bit is not set)
;; 
;; There are more combinations but these are the most common.
;; 
;; op2: condition register field and condition bit
;; 
;; This contains an immediate value indicating which condition field
;; to read and what bits to test. Each field is 4 bits long with CR0
;; at bit 0, CR1 at bit 4, etc. The value is computed as 4*CR+condition
;; with these condition values:
;; 
;; 0 -> LT
;; 1 -> GT
;; 2 -> EQ
;; 3 -> OVG
;; 
;; Thus 0 means test CR0 for LT, 5 means CR1 for GT, 30 means CR7 for EQ.
;; 
;; op3: branch target
;; 
;; Examples:
;; 
;; BC 12, 0, target		<=>	blt cr0, target
;; BC 12, 2, target		<=>	beq cr0, target
;; BC 12, 5, target		<=>	bgt cr1, target
;; BC 12, 30, target		<=>	beq cr7, target
;; BC 4, 6, target		<=>	bne cr1, target
;; BC 4, 1, target		<=>	ble cr1, target
;; 
;; The following extended opcodes are available for ease of use and readability:
;; 
;; BNE CR2, target		<=>	bne cr2, target
;; BEQ CR4, target		<=>	beq cr4, target
;; BLT target			<=>	blt target (cr0 default)
;; BGE CR7, target		<=>	bge cr7, target
;; 
;; Refer to the ISA for more information on additional values for the BC instruction,
;; how to handle OVG information, and much more.
;; 
;; 5. Align directive
;; 
;; Starting with Go 1.12, Go asm supports the PCALIGN directive, which indicates
;; that the next instruction should be aligned to the specified value. Currently
;; 8 and 16 are the only supported values, and a maximum of 2 NOPs will be added
;; to align the code. That means in the case where the code is aligned to 4 but
;; PCALIGN $16 is at that location, the code will only be aligned to 8 to avoid
;; adding 3 NOPs.
;; 
;; The purpose of this directive is to improve performance for cases like loops
;; where better alignment (8 or 16 instead of 4) might be helpful. This directive
;; exists in PPC64 assembler and is frequently used by PPC64 assembler writers.
;; 
;; PCALIGN $16
;; PCALIGN $8
;; 
;; By default, functions in Go are aligned to 16 bytes, as is the case in all
;; other compilers for PPC64. If there is a PCALIGN directive requesting alignment
;; greater than 16, then the alignment of the containing function must be
;; promoted to that same alignment or greater.
;; 
;; The behavior of PCALIGN is changed in Go 1.21 to be more straightforward to
;; ensure the alignment required for some instructions in power10. The acceptable
;; values are 8, 16, 32 and 64, and the use of those values will always provide the
;; specified alignment.
;; 
;; 6. Shift instructions
;; 
;; The simple scalar shifts on PPC64 expect a shift count that fits in 5 bits for
;; 32-bit values or 6 bit for 64-bit values. If the shift count is a constant value
;; greater than the max then the assembler sets it to the max for that size (31 for
;; 32 bit values, 63 for 64 bit values). If the shift count is in a register, then
;; only the low 5 or 6 bits of the register will be used as the shift count. The
;; Go compiler will add appropriate code to compare the shift value to achieve the
;; correct result, and the assembler does not add extra checking.
;; 
;; Examples:
;; 
;; SRAD $8,R3,R4		=>	sradi r4,r3,8
;; SRD $8,R3,R4		=>	rldicl r4,r3,56,8
;; SLD $8,R3,R4		=>	rldicr r4,r3,8,55
;; SRAW $16,R4,R5		=>	srawi r5,r4,16
;; SRW $40,R4,R5		=>	rlwinm r5,r4,0,0,31
;; SLW $12,R4,R5		=>	rlwinm r5,r4,12,0,19
;; 
;; Some non-simple shifts have operands in the Go assembly which don't map directly
;; onto operands in the PPC64 assembly. When an operand in a shift instruction in the
;; Go assembly is a bit mask, that mask is represented as a start and end bit in the
;; PPC64 assembly instead of a mask. See the ISA for more detail on these types of shifts.
;; Here are a few examples:
;; 
;; RLWMI $7,R3,$65535,R6 	=>	rlwimi r6,r3,7,16,31
;; RLDMI $0,R4,$7,R6 		=>	rldimi r6,r4,0,61
;; 
;; More recently, Go opcodes were added which map directly onto the PPC64 opcodes. It is
;; recommended to use the newer opcodes to avoid confusion.
;; 
;; RLDICL $0,R4,$15,R6		=>	rldicl r6,r4,0,15
;; RLDICR $0,R4,$15,R6		=>	rldicr r6.r4,0,15
;; 
;; # Register naming
;; 
;; 1. Special register usage in Go asm
;; 
;; The following registers should not be modified by user Go assembler code.
;; 
;; R0: Go code expects this register to contain the value 0.
;; R1: Stack pointer
;; R2: TOC pointer when compiled with -shared or -dynlink (a.k.a position independent code)
;; R13: TLS pointer
;; R30: g (goroutine)
;; 
;; Register names:
;; 
;; Rn is used for general purpose registers. (0-31)
;; Fn is used for floating point registers. (0-31)
;; Vn is used for vector registers. Slot 0 of Vn overlaps with Fn. (0-31)
;; VSn is used for vector-scalar registers. V0-V31 overlap with VS32-VS63. (0-63)
;; CTR represents the count register.
;; LR represents the link register.
;; CR represents the condition register
;; CRn represents a condition register field. (0-7)
;; CRnLT represents CR bit 0 of CR field n. (0-7)
;; CRnGT represents CR bit 1 of CR field n. (0-7)
;; CRnEQ represents CR bit 2 of CR field n. (0-7)
;; CRnSO represents CR bit 3 of CR field n. (0-7)
;; 
;; # GOPPC64 >= power10 and its effects on Go asm
;; 
;; When GOPPC64=power10 is used to compile a Go program for ppc64le/linux, MOV*, FMOV*, and ADD
;; opcodes which would require 2 or more machine instructions to emulate a 32 bit constant, or
;; symbolic reference are implemented using prefixed instructions.
;; 
;; A user who wishes granular control over the generated machine code is advised to use Go asm
;; opcodes which explicitly translate to one PPC64 machine instruction. Most common opcodes
;; are supported.
;; 
;; Some examples of how pseudo-op assembly changes with GOPPC64:
;; 
;; Go asm                       GOPPC64 <= power9          GOPPC64 >= power10
;; MOVD mypackage·foo(SB), R3   addis r2, r3, ...          pld r3, ...
;; ld    r3, r3, ...
;; 
;; MOVD 131072(R3), R4          addis r31, r4, 2           pld r4, 131072(r3)
;; ld    r4, 0(R3)
;; 
;; ADD $131073, R3              lis  r31, 2                paddi r3, r3, 131073
;; addi r31, 1
;; add  r3,r31,r3
;; 
;; MOVD $131073, R3             lis  r3, 2                 pli r3, 131073
;; addi r3, 1
;; 
;; MOVD $mypackage·foo(SB), R3  addis r2, r3, ...          pla r3, ...
;; addi  r3, r3, ...
;; */
(in-ns 'go.cmd.internal.obj.ppc64) (go/file "doc.go" :doc "Package ppc64 implements a PPC64 assembler that assembles Go asm into\nthe corresponding PPC64 instructions as defined by the Power ISA 3.0B.\n\nThis document provides information on how to write code in Go assembler\nfor PPC64, focusing on the differences between Go and PPC64 assembly language.\nIt assumes some knowledge of PPC64 assembler. The original implementation of\nPPC64 in Go defined many opcodes that are different from PPC64 opcodes, but\nupdates to the Go assembly language used mnemonics that are mostly similar if not\nidentical to the PPC64 mneumonics, such as VMX and VSX instructions. Not all detail\nis included here; refer to the Power ISA document if interested in more detail.\n\nStarting with Go 1.15 the Go objdump supports the -gnu option, which provides a\nside by side view of the Go assembler and the PPC64 assembler output. This is\nextremely helpful in determining what final PPC64 assembly is generated from the\ncorresponding Go assembly.\n\nIn the examples below, the Go assembly is on the left, PPC64 assembly on the right.\n\n1. Operand ordering\n\nIn Go asm, the last operand (right) is the target operand, but with PPC64 asm,\nthe first operand (left) is the target. The order of the remaining operands is\nnot consistent: in general opcodes with 3 operands that perform math or logical\noperations have their operands in reverse order. Opcodes for vector instructions\nand those with more than 3 operands usually have operands in the same order except\nfor the target operand, which is first in PPC64 asm and last in Go asm.\n\nExample:\n\n\tADD R3, R4, R5\t\t<=>\tadd r5, r4, r3\n\n2. Constant operands\n\nIn Go asm, an operand that starts with '$' indicates a constant value. If the\ninstruction using the constant has an immediate version of the opcode, then an\nimmediate value is used with the opcode if possible.\n\nExample:\n\n\tADD $1, R3, R4\t\t<=> \taddi r4, r3, 1\n\n3. Opcodes setting condition codes\n\nIn PPC64 asm, some instructions other than compares have variations that can set\nthe condition code where meaningful. This is indicated by adding '.' to the end\nof the PPC64 instruction. In Go asm, these instructions have 'CC' at the end of\nthe opcode. The possible settings of the condition code depend on the instruction.\nCR0 is the default for fixed-point instructions; CR1 for floating point; CR6 for\nvector instructions.\n\nExample:\n\n\tANDCC R3, R4, R5\t\t<=>\tand. r5, r3, r4 (set CR0)\n\n4. Loads and stores from memory\n\nIn Go asm, opcodes starting with 'MOV' indicate a load or store. When the target\nis a memory reference, then it is a store; when the target is a register and the\nsource is a memory reference, then it is a load.\n\nMOV{B,H,W,D} variations identify the size as byte, halfword, word, doubleword.\n\nAdding 'Z' to the opcode for a load indicates zero extend; if omitted it is sign extend.\nAdding 'U' to a load or store indicates an update of the base register with the offset.\nAdding 'BR' to an opcode indicates byte-reversed load or store, or the order opposite\nof the expected endian order. If 'BR' is used then zero extend is assumed.\n\nMemory references n(Ra) indicate the address in Ra + n. When used with an update form\nof an opcode, the value in Ra is incremented by n.\n\nMemory references (Ra+Rb) or (Ra)(Rb) indicate the address Ra + Rb, used by indexed\nloads or stores. Both forms are accepted. When used with an update then the base register\nis updated by the value in the index register.\n\nExamples:\n\n\tMOVD (R3), R4\t\t<=>\tld r4,0(r3)\n\tMOVW (R3), R4\t\t<=>\tlwa r4,0(r3)\n\tMOVWZU 4(R3), R4\t\t<=>\tlwzu r4,4(r3)\n\tMOVWZ (R3+R5), R4\t\t<=>\tlwzx r4,r3,r5\n\tMOVHZ  (R3), R4\t\t<=>\tlhz r4,0(r3)\n\tMOVHU 2(R3), R4\t\t<=>\tlhau r4,2(r3)\n\tMOVBZ (R3), R4\t\t<=>\tlbz r4,0(r3)\n\n\tMOVD R4,(R3)\t\t<=>\tstd r4,0(r3)\n\tMOVW R4,(R3)\t\t<=>\tstw r4,0(r3)\n\tMOVW R4,(R3+R5)\t\t<=>\tstwx r4,r3,r5\n\tMOVWU R4,4(R3)\t\t<=>\tstwu r4,4(r3)\n\tMOVH R4,2(R3)\t\t<=>\tsth r4,2(r3)\n\tMOVBU R4,(R3)(R5)\t\t<=>\tstbux r4,r3,r5\n\n4. Compares\n\nWhen an instruction does a compare or other operation that might\nresult in a condition code, then the resulting condition is set\nin a field of the condition register. The condition register consists\nof 8 4-bit fields named CR0 - CR7. When a compare instruction\nidentifies a CR then the resulting condition is set in that field\nto be read by a later branch or isel instruction. Within these fields,\nbits are set to indicate less than, greater than, or equal conditions.\n\nOnce an instruction sets a condition, then a subsequent branch, isel or\nother instruction can read the condition field and operate based on the\nbit settings.\n\nExamples:\n\n\tCMP R3, R4\t\t\t<=>\tcmp r3, r4\t(CR0 assumed)\n\tCMP R3, R4, CR1\t\t<=>\tcmp cr1, r3, r4\n\nNote that the condition register is the target operand of compare opcodes, so\nthe remaining operands are in the same order for Go asm and PPC64 asm.\nWhen CR0 is used then it is implicit and does not need to be specified.\n\n5. Branches\n\nMany branches are represented as a form of the BC instruction. There are\nother extended opcodes to make it easier to see what type of branch is being\nused.\n\nThe following is a brief description of the BC instruction and its commonly\nused operands.\n\nBC op1, op2, op3\n\n\t  op1: type of branch\n\t      16 -> bctr (branch on ctr)\n\t      12 -> bcr  (branch if cr bit is set)\n\t      8  -> bcr+bctr (branch on ctr and cr values)\n\t\t4  -> bcr != 0 (branch if specified cr bit is not set)\n\n\t\tThere are more combinations but these are the most common.\n\n\t  op2: condition register field and condition bit\n\n\t\tThis contains an immediate value indicating which condition field\n\t\tto read and what bits to test. Each field is 4 bits long with CR0\n\t      at bit 0, CR1 at bit 4, etc. The value is computed as 4*CR+condition\n\t      with these condition values:\n\n\t      0 -> LT\n\t      1 -> GT\n\t      2 -> EQ\n\t      3 -> OVG\n\n\t\tThus 0 means test CR0 for LT, 5 means CR1 for GT, 30 means CR7 for EQ.\n\n\t  op3: branch target\n\nExamples:\n\n\tBC 12, 0, target\t\t<=>\tblt cr0, target\n\tBC 12, 2, target\t\t<=>\tbeq cr0, target\n\tBC 12, 5, target\t\t<=>\tbgt cr1, target\n\tBC 12, 30, target\t\t<=>\tbeq cr7, target\n\tBC 4, 6, target\t\t<=>\tbne cr1, target\n\tBC 4, 1, target\t\t<=>\tble cr1, target\n\n\tThe following extended opcodes are available for ease of use and readability:\n\n\tBNE CR2, target\t\t<=>\tbne cr2, target\n\tBEQ CR4, target\t\t<=>\tbeq cr4, target\n\tBLT target\t\t\t<=>\tblt target (cr0 default)\n\tBGE CR7, target\t\t<=>\tbge cr7, target\n\nRefer to the ISA for more information on additional values for the BC instruction,\nhow to handle OVG information, and much more.\n\n5. Align directive\n\nStarting with Go 1.12, Go asm supports the PCALIGN directive, which indicates\nthat the next instruction should be aligned to the specified value. Currently\n8 and 16 are the only supported values, and a maximum of 2 NOPs will be added\nto align the code. That means in the case where the code is aligned to 4 but\nPCALIGN $16 is at that location, the code will only be aligned to 8 to avoid\nadding 3 NOPs.\n\nThe purpose of this directive is to improve performance for cases like loops\nwhere better alignment (8 or 16 instead of 4) might be helpful. This directive\nexists in PPC64 assembler and is frequently used by PPC64 assembler writers.\n\nPCALIGN $16\nPCALIGN $8\n\nBy default, functions in Go are aligned to 16 bytes, as is the case in all\nother compilers for PPC64. If there is a PCALIGN directive requesting alignment\ngreater than 16, then the alignment of the containing function must be\npromoted to that same alignment or greater.\n\nThe behavior of PCALIGN is changed in Go 1.21 to be more straightforward to\nensure the alignment required for some instructions in power10. The acceptable\nvalues are 8, 16, 32 and 64, and the use of those values will always provide the\nspecified alignment.\n\n6. Shift instructions\n\nThe simple scalar shifts on PPC64 expect a shift count that fits in 5 bits for\n32-bit values or 6 bit for 64-bit values. If the shift count is a constant value\ngreater than the max then the assembler sets it to the max for that size (31 for\n32 bit values, 63 for 64 bit values). If the shift count is in a register, then\nonly the low 5 or 6 bits of the register will be used as the shift count. The\nGo compiler will add appropriate code to compare the shift value to achieve the\ncorrect result, and the assembler does not add extra checking.\n\nExamples:\n\n\tSRAD $8,R3,R4\t\t=>\tsradi r4,r3,8\n\tSRD $8,R3,R4\t\t=>\trldicl r4,r3,56,8\n\tSLD $8,R3,R4\t\t=>\trldicr r4,r3,8,55\n\tSRAW $16,R4,R5\t\t=>\tsrawi r5,r4,16\n\tSRW $40,R4,R5\t\t=>\trlwinm r5,r4,0,0,31\n\tSLW $12,R4,R5\t\t=>\trlwinm r5,r4,12,0,19\n\nSome non-simple shifts have operands in the Go assembly which don't map directly\nonto operands in the PPC64 assembly. When an operand in a shift instruction in the\nGo assembly is a bit mask, that mask is represented as a start and end bit in the\nPPC64 assembly instead of a mask. See the ISA for more detail on these types of shifts.\nHere are a few examples:\n\n\tRLWMI $7,R3,$65535,R6 \t=>\trlwimi r6,r3,7,16,31\n\tRLDMI $0,R4,$7,R6 \t\t=>\trldimi r6,r4,0,61\n\nMore recently, Go opcodes were added which map directly onto the PPC64 opcodes. It is\nrecommended to use the newer opcodes to avoid confusion.\n\n\tRLDICL $0,R4,$15,R6\t\t=>\trldicl r6,r4,0,15\n\tRLDICR $0,R4,$15,R6\t\t=>\trldicr r6.r4,0,15\n\n# Register naming\n\n1. Special register usage in Go asm\n\nThe following registers should not be modified by user Go assembler code.\n\n\tR0: Go code expects this register to contain the value 0.\n\tR1: Stack pointer\n\tR2: TOC pointer when compiled with -shared or -dynlink (a.k.a position independent code)\n\tR13: TLS pointer\n\tR30: g (goroutine)\n\nRegister names:\n\n\tRn is used for general purpose registers. (0-31)\n\tFn is used for floating point registers. (0-31)\n\tVn is used for vector registers. Slot 0 of Vn overlaps with Fn. (0-31)\n\tVSn is used for vector-scalar registers. V0-V31 overlap with VS32-VS63. (0-63)\n\tCTR represents the count register.\n\tLR represents the link register.\n\tCR represents the condition register\n\tCRn represents a condition register field. (0-7)\n\tCRnLT represents CR bit 0 of CR field n. (0-7)\n\tCRnGT represents CR bit 1 of CR field n. (0-7)\n\tCRnEQ represents CR bit 2 of CR field n. (0-7)\n\tCRnSO represents CR bit 3 of CR field n. (0-7)\n\n# GOPPC64 >= power10 and its effects on Go asm\n\nWhen GOPPC64=power10 is used to compile a Go program for ppc64le/linux, MOV*, FMOV*, and ADD\nopcodes which would require 2 or more machine instructions to emulate a 32 bit constant, or\nsymbolic reference are implemented using prefixed instructions.\n\nA user who wishes granular control over the generated machine code is advised to use Go asm\nopcodes which explicitly translate to one PPC64 machine instruction. Most common opcodes\nare supported.\n\nSome examples of how pseudo-op assembly changes with GOPPC64:\n\n\tGo asm                       GOPPC64 <= power9          GOPPC64 >= power10\n\tMOVD mypackage·foo(SB), R3   addis r2, r3, ...          pld r3, ...\n\t                             ld    r3, r3, ...\n\n\tMOVD 131072(R3), R4          addis r31, r4, 2           pld r4, 131072(r3)\n\t                             ld    r4, 0(R3)\n\n\tADD $131073, R3              lis  r31, 2                paddi r3, r3, 131073\n\t                             addi r31, 1\n\t                             add  r3,r31,r3\n\n\tMOVD $131073, R3             lis  r3, 2                 pli r3, 131073\n\t                             addi r3, 1\n\n\tMOVD $mypackage·foo(SB), R3  addis r2, r3, ...          pla r3, ...\n\t                             addi  r3, r3, ...\n" :imports [])
